  "title": "P1×3L",
  "summary": "A P1&times;3L is the atomic unit of digital and generative art. It can&rsquo;t be quantified. A P1&times;3L is vibrant and alive. Artists choreograph P1&times;3Ls and P1&times;3Ls possess artists. P1&times;3Ls are ideas about image-making that transcend the technology they flow through.\n\n<hr style=\"height: 1px;border: 0;border-top: 1px solid #c4c4c4;\">\nI&rsquo;m thrilled to bring together Nicolas Sassoon, Francoise Gamma, Andreas Gysin (aka ertdfgcvb), Raquel Meyers, and p1xelfool for the P1&times;3L exhibition. This is a remarkable group of artists, and each has pushed the boundaries of the fields of digital and generative art in their own ways. As a counterpoint to the stream of technology being developed to make itself invisible, these artists foreground the materiality of their medium. I&rsquo;ve been looking at their work on sites across the network for a long time, and I felt it was necessary to see it all together.\n\nEach of the artists in the P1&times;3L exhibition use digital media at the core of their practice, but many also have experience making art prior to working with software. When I was speaking with Raquel last month, she began the conversation with a story about her early experiences with photography. Vision through an analog camera of this era is a hybrid analog/digital sight&mdash; looking at the world through a glass lens, but with the image divided into crisp grid units. It doesn&rsquo;t take a stretch of the imagination to see how this early experience informed Rachel&rsquo;s work from the last decade, in which she creates uniform grids of text and symbols with vintage Commodore 64 computers and Teletext systems. She performs these images live by adding text and symbols from the upper-left corner of a computer screen to the lower right, one symbol at a time. The machines focus her energy and attention &mdash; they require her complete concentration. There&rsquo;s no undo and no distraction from social media notifications or emails coming th...",
